import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Generated/Paths

## The otel package: setting it up, every request timed by its route, the app's own counters,
## histograms and gauges, where they are sent, the server's JSON log for a collector, how it works,
## and what it doesn't do yet.
## Static: a collector is a server.
meta = {
	title: "OpenTelemetry",
	description: "The otel package for Roc Solid apps: metrics sent over OTLP to any OpenTelemetry Collector or backend, every request timed by its route, the app's own counters, histograms and gauges, and the server's JSON log for a collector to read.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "setup", label: "Setting up" },
		{ id: "requests", label: "Every request, timed" },
		{ id: "instruments", label: "Measuring your own" },
		{ id: "where", label: "Where they go" },
		{ id: "logs", label: "Logs" },
		{ id: "how", label: "How it works" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/otel</code>]}
				title="Metrics and logs, for any OpenTelemetry backend"
			>
				Time every request by its route, count whatever your app cares about, and send it all over OTLP to
				an OpenTelemetry Collector or to a backend that accepts it (Grafana Cloud, Honeycomb, Datadog, and
				others). For logs, a collector reads the server's JSON log; the app adds nothing.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				Use this package when you run an app in production and want to see how it behaves: how long
				requests take, which routes are slow or failing, and numbers of your own, such as signups or
				checks that failed. It speaks OpenTelemetry, the standard most monitoring tools accept, and it
				is configured with the same environment variables as every OpenTelemetry SDK. With no collector
				configured it does nothing, so it costs nothing while you develop.
			</p>
			<p>
				The <a href={Paths.examples_uptime}>uptime example</a> uses it: a histogram of each check's latency,
				and a gauge of whether each monitor is up.
			</p>

			<Heading id="setup">Setting up</Heading>
			<ol>
				<li>Name the package in the app's header: <code>otel: "…/packages/otel/main.roc"</code>.</li>
				<li>
					List its plugin, <code>Otel.plugin({"{}"})</code>, in the program's <code>plugins</code>, and
					<code>import otel.Otel</code> where you record. Nothing is copied into the app.
				</li>
				<li>Put <code>Otel.middleware({"{}"})</code> first in the app's middleware.</li>
				<li>
					Start the server with <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code> set to your collector, such as
					<code>http://localhost:4318</code>.
				</li>
			</ol>

			<Heading id="requests">Every request, timed</Heading>
			<Code
				title="main.roc, in part"
				source={"program = App.program({\n\tinit!: |started| Ok({ facts: started }),\n\tmiddleware: Otel.middleware({}).then(Middlewares.default),\n\tplugins: [Otel.plugin({})],\n})"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				<code>Otel.middleware({"{}"})</code> times every request that passes through it. It records
				<code>http.server.request.duration</code>, a histogram in seconds, with the attributes
				OpenTelemetry's HTTP conventions name:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><code>http.request.method</code>, or <code>_OTHER</code> for a method HTTP doesn't define;</li>
				<li>
					<code>http.route</code>, the route's pattern (<code>/posts/[id]</code>, never
					<code>/posts/42</code>, so each post doesn't get a series of its own), left out when no route
					matched;
				</li>
				<li><code>http.response.status_code</code>.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Put it first in the <a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>middleware</a> chain, so its time
				includes the rest of the middleware too.
			</p>

			<Heading id="instruments">Measuring your own</Heading>
			<p>
				An instrument is something your app measures. Define one with <code>Metric</code>, then record
				values with <code>Otel.record!</code> anywhere on the server, with attributes that label the value:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="Anywhere on the server"
				source={"signups = Metric.counter({ name: \"app.signups\", description: \"Members who signed up.\", unit: \"{member}\" })\nOtel.record!(signups, 1, [(\"plan\", \"pro\")])\n\ncheckout = Metric.histogram({ name: \"app.checkout.duration\", description: \"Checkout.\", unit: \"s\", buckets: Metric.seconds })\nOtel.record!(checkout, seconds, [(\"method\", \"card\")])\n\nup = Metric.gauge({ name: \"app.service.up\", description: \"Whether the service answered (1) or not (0).\", unit: \"1\" })\nOtel.record!(up, 1, [(\"service\", \"payments\")])"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<ul>
				<li>
					A <strong>counter</strong> adds up what it is given, such as signups or emails sent. It never goes
					down: a negative value is dropped.
				</li>
				<li>
					A <strong>histogram</strong> counts values into buckets and sums them, such as how long a
					checkout took. <code>Metric.seconds</code> is OpenTelemetry's buckets for durations, from 5 ms to
					10 s.
				</li>
				<li>
					A <strong>gauge</strong> keeps the last value set, negative values too, such as whether a service
					is up or how many jobs are waiting. It is sent again at each export until it is set to another
					value.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Each combination of attribute values is a separate series, so keep the values few. A user's id or
				a full URL as an attribute would make a series per user or per URL. To protect memory, an
				instrument keeps at most 2000 series and drops new ones past that. One name is one kind of
				instrument: a value recorded under a name already used by another kind is dropped.
			</p>

			<Heading id="where">Where they go</Heading>
			<p>
				The package reads the standard environment variables, as every OpenTelemetry SDK does:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>What it sets</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code></td><td>the collector's address; <code>/v1/metrics</code> is added</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS</code></td><td>headers to send, <code>name=value,…</code>, such as a backend's key</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>OTEL_SERVICE_NAME</code>, <code>OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES</code></td><td>the service's name and attributes</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>OTEL_METRIC_EXPORT_INTERVAL</code></td><td>milliseconds between sends (60000)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>OTEL_SDK_DISABLED=true</code></td><td>turns it off</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				With no endpoint, metrics are off: nothing is measured, kept or sent. That is the default while
				you develop. The package's README lists the other variables it reads (a metrics-only endpoint and
				headers, the send timeout, <code>OTEL_METRICS_EXPORTER=none</code>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="logs">Logs</Heading>
			<p>
				Logs need nothing from this package. When the server's <a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#logging"}>log</a>
				is JSON, each line is one object, the platform's lines and the app's alike. The log is JSON when
				stderr isn't a terminal (where a log is collected), when <code>ROCX_LOG=json</code> is set, or when
				the program says <code>{"logs: { format: Json }"}</code>. The OpenTelemetry Collector's
				<code>filelog</code> receiver turns each line into a log record: its time, its level as the
				severity, its message as the body, and its fields as attributes. The package's README has the
				collector's configuration.
			</p>

			<Heading id="how">How it works</Heading>
			<p>
				Recording a value puts it in the server's memory (<a href={Paths.platform_kv}>Kv</a>), so a request
				never waits for it. The plugin's loop takes what is there each second and adds it to the sums it
				keeps; every export interval, and once more as the server stops, it sends them all in one request,
				as OTLP over HTTP, in JSON.
			</p>
			<p>
				The sums are cumulative: each send says what has added up since the loop started. So a send that
				fails loses nothing; the next one carries it all, and the failure is logged as a warning. Nothing is
				written to the database: a restart starts the sums again, with a new start time, which a collector
				reads as a reset counter.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Traces.</strong> They need to know which request a query or a fetch runs for, without the
					app passing it by hand, and Roc has no such context yet.
				</li>
				<li>Up-down counters, exemplars, and Prometheus's <code>/metrics</code> for pulling.</li>
				<li>A job's duration and outcome.</li>
				<li>Metrics kept in memory alone, without a database write per value.</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
